~ core.test — what a test harness and its reporter are built from. Import with
~ `<< core.test`. For the harness and output Quilon ships, import `<< core.test.report`
~ instead. Reference: docs/corelib/test.md.
~
~ ── Assertions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ `assert` and `expect` are provided by the COMPILER, so they need no import and are not
~ defined here. Both take the value under test and a matcher:
~
~   assert(2 + 2, equals(4))    report at YOUR call site and exit 101 on failure
~   expect(2 + 2, equals(4))    report, mark the case failed, and carry on (tests only)
~
~ The matchers are `equals`, `contains`, `not`, `isOk`, `isNotOk` — see
~ docs/corelib/test.md.
~
~   failAt(message)       fail outright at the CALLER's location, the way `assert` does —
~                         what a check of your own is built from. Take a trailing
~                         `site :: Site` and forward it to report ITS caller:
~                           assertEven = (n :: Num, site :: Site) -> $ =>
~                             n % 2 == 0 ? $ : failAt("`n` is odd", site)
~
~ ── The run's state, and the case lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────
~ `quilon test` runs a file's top-level `describe` blocks and ends by calling
~ `reportSummary()`, whose result is the run's status — 0 passes the suite, anything else
~ fails it. Neither `describe`,
~ `it`, nor any `report*` function lives here: they are what `core.test.report` builds
~ out of the functions below, and what a harness of your own defines instead.
~
~ Everything the run records is reachable through these — a reporter never names a
~ registry primitive:
~
~   casesPassed()  casesFailed()  nestingDepth()      what the run has recorded
~   enterSuite()   leaveSuite()                       open and close a group
~   caseFailing()  finishCase()                       ask about, then close, a case
<< core.io

~ Report `message` at `site` — the location of the call that left the `site` argument off —
~ in the compiler's diagnostic frame, then exit 101.
>> failAt = (message :: Text, site :: Site) -> $ => <
  color = __color_enabled(stderr)
  position = color ? "\e[36m" : ""
  problem = color ? "\e[1;31m" : ""
  frame = color ? "\e[2m" : ""
  plain = color ? "\e[0m" : ""

  number = "`site.line`"
  gutter = " ".repeat(number.length)
  lead = " ".repeat(site.column - 1)
  carets = "^".repeat(site.width)

  ~ Keep in step with `shorten_path` in the runtime, which shortens a path the same way.
  room = 60
  file = site.file.length > room
    ? "…" + site.file.slice(site.file.length - room + 1, site.file.length)
    : site.file

  eprint("`position``file`:`site.line`:`site.column`:`plain`")
  eprint("`problem``message``plain`")
  eprint("`frame``gutter` |`plain`")
  eprint("`frame``number` |`plain` `site.excerpt`")
  eprint("`frame``gutter` |`plain` `lead``problem``carets``plain`")
  __exit(101)
>

~ ── The run's state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

~ How many cases have run with no failing `expect`.
>> casesPassed = () -> Num => __test_passed()

~ How many cases have run with at least one failing `expect`. Non-zero is what makes a
~ run exit non-zero.
>> casesFailed = () -> Num => __test_failed()

~ How many `describe` groups are open: 0 outside any group, 1 inside an outermost one.
>> nestingDepth = () -> Num => __test_depth()

~ ── The case lifecycle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ What a `describe` and an `it` of your own call, in this order:
~   enterSuite() … the group's body … leaveSuite()
~   the case's body … caseFailing() … finishCase()

~ Open a group; yields the depth it sits at (1 for an outermost group), which is what a
~ reporter indents by.
>> enterSuite = () -> Num => __test_suite_enter()

~ Close the group just entered; yields the depth that remains.
>> leaveSuite = () -> Num => __test_suite_leave()

~ Whether the case now running has already failed an `expect`. Ask BEFORE closing it —
~ closing clears the mark for the next case.
>> caseFailing = () -> Bool => __test_case_failing() == 1

~ Close the case that just ran, tallying it as passed or failed; yields the depth to
~ report it at.
>> finishCase = () -> Num => __test_case_finish()
